  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "O caderno marcado",
    "overview": "FACODI reads like a student's annotated notebook, not a streaming feed. The page is warm paper, the text is navy ink, and the sun-yellow marker is the one loud gesture: it highlights the word that matters, the active filter and the next action. Coloured tabs, like index stickers on a binder, tell content types apart without competing with the marker.\n\nLearners mostly arrive on a phone, wanting to find a unit and start studying, so the interface is calm, high-contrast and flat. Editorial and account pages use the same world with denser lists and typed forms. Real thumbnails of curated videos are the only imagery; the curriculum itself is the hero.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Warm paper canvas with navy ink, light by default and with a deep-navy dark mode.",
      "One sun-yellow marker for emphasis, active state and primary actions.",
      "Square geometry everywhere; borders separate, shadows barely exist.",
      "Coloured tabs encode content type, always with an icon and a written label.",
      "Navy ink bands opened by a 4px sun rule mark the footer and key panels.",
      "Space Grotesk for reading, IBM Plex Mono for labels, counts and metadata."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Marker Rule",
        "body": "Sun yellow means \"look here\" or \"act here\". Never use it as a section background or a decorative wash.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Navy-on-Fill Rule",
        "body": "Sun and every marker tab carry navy text, at least 5.7:1. White text never sits on a light fill; sun text is allowed only on ink.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Label Names It Rule",
        "body": "Content-type and status colours are decoration. The badge text always names the type or state.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Neutral Status Rule",
